{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1355756-are-m20-s-rules-about-multiple-effects-new-have-i-just-been-playing-wrong-for-years","id":1355756,"title":"Are M20's Rules about multiple effects NEW? Have I just been playing wrong for years?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-12-18T20:55:29-08:00","id":1355756,"title":"Are M20's Rules about multiple effects NEW? Have I just been playing wrong for years?","content":"<b>On pgs. 528-529 of M20</b>, it discusses how many effects you can use at once:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>How Many Effects Can I Use at Once?</b></span><br /> A mage can cast only one Effect per turn, even if she\u2019s using Time 3 magick to speed up her activities. She may, however, keep any number of Effects running at a time, although it becomes more and more difficult for her to do so. Game-wise, you add +1 to your difficulty for every two Effects you have running at one time \u2013 that is, +1 difficulty for two Effects, +2 for four Effects, +3 for six Effects, and so on. In this case, the difficulty can go higher than the usual limit of +3\u2026 which makes it damn near impossible to juggle a large number of simultaneous Effects. As an overall note, an Effect that has a Time-based trigger, one which has been locked into another Pattern, or one that has been cast but whose duration has not yet expired, does not count toward that total. If Lee Ann enchants a guy, and if \u2013 thanks to the number of successes rolled \u2013 that enchantment lasts for a week after they part company, then Lee Ann does not have to concentrate on the Effect in order to keep it going. If she wishes to keep the Effect in motion, however, then it counts against the number of Effects that character can employ at the same time. </div> </div> </div>Okay... has this always been the rule? Per my reading of prior editions (really just Rev. and 2nd Ed.) the way I was running it was that <i>even effects on which you'd spent successes for duration would still count towards the penalty--i.e., were &quot;Hanging&quot;.</i> I exempted from the rule any effect &quot;suspended&quot; with Time 4 (because having to &quot;hang&quot; that kind of effect seemed to frustrate the underlying purpose of the Time 4 effect). I went back and I read the write-up in Revised for this, starting on <b>pg. 155 labeled &quot;Simultaneous Effects&quot;</b>, and I really gotta wonder whether I merely misinterpreted what (only now) appears to be an ambiguous/vague rule open to many reasonable interpretations, or whether M20 straight up just changed this rule altogether:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>Simultaneous Effects</b></span><br /> Although a mage can cost only one Effect at a time, he can keep various Effects running. The difficulty of doing so often varies with the Effect's type.<br /> <br /> A simple Effect that just modifies the mage or surroundings fora time requires only a tiny flow of the mage\u2019s attention and magical effort. Keeping the Effect moving is a constant push from the Avatar and the will, but it's a small one, since the Effect is generally somewhat self-sustaining or static. Such Effects include things like body-enhancement, sensory improvements or even small changes to Patterns that are designed to last for only a short time. Such simple Effects cost you a difficulty penalty of one for every two full Effects in use whenever your mage tries to cast a new Effect.<br /> <br /> More complex Effects like mind reading, juggling huge Forces or manipulating Life Patterns all require the mage\u2019s concentration. These Effects require constant update and manipulation, so the mage must divert a substantial amount of Awakened will to them. Your mage may not be able to concentrate enough to perform other Effects while doing something this delicate, at the Storyteller's discretion. If the Storyteller lets you concentrate on multiple Effects (or if your character has specific Merits or magical Effects that let him maintain multiple areas of concentration), you\u2018ll still take a difficulty penalty of one for every two Effects that your mage has running. That\u2019s for simple and complex Effects both.<br /> <br /> Instant Effects rewrite Patterns or alter reality and then are done; they require no further maintenance. If you change a material into a different sort permanently, or you create something from nothing and give it Prime energy to make it fully real and permanent, then it's part of the Tapestry. Such manifestations no longer require concentration. </div> </div> </div>Second Edition is even more brief, and the most open to interpretation! From the friggin' <i>blurb </i>on the matter on <b>page 161 of 2nd Ed. Core</b>:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>Simultaneous Effects</b></span><br /> A mage can cast only one effect per turn, even if she has used Time magicks to speed herself up (reality is already &quot;preoccupied&quot; when it's in a different time frame). She can keep as many Effects &quot;running&quot; at one time as she wants, though it becomes more and more difficult to do (+1 additional difficulty for every two Effects in use). </div> </div> </div>Sorcerer's Crusade is equally brief; from <b>pg. 226 of <i>Mage: The Sorcerer's Crusade</i></b>:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>Simultaneous Workings</b></span><br /> A sorcerer may cast only one spell per turn, no matter how quickly she may he moving. (See the Time Sphere.) She can keep as many spells &quot;running&quot; as she wants; after a while, however, it gets difficult to concentrate on them all ( + 1 to the casting difficulty for every two spells in use), </div> </div> </div>Seriously, it feels like M20 really blew the lid off this whole thing. I had made a house rule to sort of soften the blow from this (the short-and-sweet of it is that you can keep a number of effects going equal to your Arete before you start taking a penalty,) but even with that rule in effect, I (and my players) have basically all gotten used to playing with a bare minimum number of effects running, and every single effect having it's own set of duration-successes to last the day or story (subject to pattern bleeding and the like of course). With the M20 rule, it looks like you could have like a dozen effects running at a time just so long as they're either locked (not a difficult thing to do), or you spent some successes on duration (also not difficult). Obviously this would mean that to people with &quot;mage senses&quot; (or Auspex 2, or whatever other effects), the mage would shine like a road flare... but still... that's really something.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":2319,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","user":"CaptOtter","date":". 12-19-2019, 12:00 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-18T22:42:26-08:00","id":1355775,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CaptOtter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1355756-are-m20-s-rules-about-multiple-effects-new-have-i-just-been-playing-wrong-for-years#post1355756\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I went back and I read the write-up in Revised for this, starting on <b>pg. 155 labeled &quot;Simultaneous Effects&quot;</b>, and I really gotta wonder whether I merely misinterpreted what (only now) appears to be an ambiguous/vague rule open to many reasonable interpretations, or whether M20 straight up just changed this rule altogether:</div> </div> </div> </div>My understanding is that M20 was attempting to make the intended meaning the Revised wording more clear, and Revised was trying to make the rule a bit more nuanced to avoid declaring anything active as &quot;running,&quot; by making concentration a factor.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-18T23:50:32-08:00","id":1355782,"title":"","content":"Yeah, to my understanding, Second and Revised preeeetty much were hard on <i>'every two effects start to cause more rote strain, period'</i>. Revised only decided to actually *up* the ante by saying that effects that require <i>full</i> concentration might not even allow you to have any other effects running at the same time at all.<br /> <br /> I'm currently playing on an online Revised game where this causes interesting implications - for example, running a ward on a sanctum. By Second and Revised rules, this passive ward counts against the 'every two effects increase difficulty' rule, unless you actually have the ward sustained by something like a wonder.<br /> <br /> The M20 ruling makes such things much more easy to handle. Passive wards, faux-artifact perma-enhancements of objects, and <b><i>effects that are locked on patterns</i></b> in general etc. all do not count against that 'running effects' threshold that increases difficulty - only effects that the Mage actively needs to concentrate on or steer in any way do. Which makes absolute sense.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 12-19-2019, 03:03 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-19T16:12:21-08:00","id":1355959,"title":"","content":"So, if I have this right, one of you (<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a>) thinks M20 merely clarified what Revised had meant to say and rule--and one of you (<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar6606_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"6606\" data-vbnamecard=\"6606\">Ambrosia</a>) thinks the rule was every bit as brutal as my original, pre-M20 interpretation.<br /> <br /> I am... right back where I started!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-19T16:51:54-08:00","id":1355965,"title":"","content":"In 1st edition, they didn\u2019t really specify details of running multiple effects. It was pretty much freeform, at ST discretion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2517-trippy","name":"Trippy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2517_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1018"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-20T03:44:18-08:00","id":1356031,"title":"","content":"Revised only states that Permanent and Instant effects don't have the penalty. Concentration effects either have that penalty or plainly forbbide anything else to be casted (which it's a far stronger penalty).<br /> <br /> M20 allows more circumstances to negate the penalty (&quot;having a Duration&quot; it's more flexible than &quot;Permanent&quot;, and also we add the Locking and Trigger thing)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}